      "id": 1,
      "text": "Anna Palm founded Palm Valley in the 1850s, turning her homestead into a thriving Swedish settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/palm-valley-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Today, travelers can wander through abandoned structures, a historic cemetery, and ruins that echo Swedish pioneer perseverance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/palm-valley-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name literally points to the palm trees that dot the subtropical Rio Grande Valley landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/palm-valley-126505",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Palm Valley sits within the McAllen‑Edinburg‑Mission metro, a region famed for citrus farming and a diversifying economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/palm-valley-126505",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A historic cemetery in Palm Valley preserves the graves of early settlers, offering a quiet glimpse into its past.",
